    {
      "id": "JbQd-IenSwI89ZDRoOG0P",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1760.1883727379522,
      "y": 3914.487352945048,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 2600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b01",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1589923310,
      "version": 2241,
      "versionNonce": 1277363588,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781442604781,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How can you support large files?\n\n2 ways to do chunking,\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Client PATCH Request\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Client chunks the file and uploads chunks directly to S3\n→ S3 responds to each chunk upload with a success message\n→ Client sends a PATCH request to backend to update the chunks field in FileMetadata\n\nPATCH /files/{fileId}/chunks\nRequest:\n{\n  \"chunks\": [\n    { \"id\": \"chunk1\", \"status\": \"uploaded\" }\n  ]\n}\n\n→ Simple to implement\n→ Real-time progress tracking on the client\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Security risk, trusting the client to keep chunks in sync\n→ Malicious user could send a PATCH request marking all chunks as uploaded without\nactually uploading them\n→ Leads to inconsistent state that is difficult to debug\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Server-Side Chunk Verification via ETags\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Each chunk gets an ETag upon successful S3 upload\n→ Client includes ETags in the PATCH request to backend\n→ Backend verifies ETags by calling S3's ListParts API — efficiently validates multiple\nchunks at once\n→ Trust but verify — accept client updates for real-time progress, but verify server-side\nbefore marking file as \"uploaded\"\n\n\n→ Balances UX with data integrity\n→ Server-side verification prevents malicious or incorrect chunk status updates\n→ ETags provide a reliable way to verify chunks without re-downloading them\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ More complex than client-only approach\n→ Requires integration with S3's multipart upload API\n\n\n🔑 Uniquely Identifying Files & Chunks\n\n🤔 The Problem\nWhen resuming an upload, two questions must be answered:\n→ 1️⃣ Have I tried to upload this file before?\n→ 2️⃣ If yes, which chunks have I already uploaded?\n→ ⚠️ Cannot rely on filename alone — two users (or same user) could upload files with\nthe same name\n\n\n🔢 Solution: Fingerprinting\n\n→ A fingerprint is a unique hash value derived from the file's content using a\ncryptographic hash function like SHA-256\n→ Same content always produces the same fingerprint — regardless of filename or\nupload source\n→ Used for deduplication (has this file been uploaded before?) and resumability (which\nchunks are done?)\n→ Fingerprint identifies content, not the file record — two users uploading the same file\nproduce the same fingerprint\n→ fileId in metadata should be a UUID (unique per record), fingerprint stored as a\nseparate field\n→ Each individual chunk also gets its own fingerprint — allows precise identification of\nwhich parts have been uploaded\n\n\n\n🔄 Full Large File Upload Flow\n\n→ 1️⃣ Client chunks file into 5-10MB pieces and calculates a fingerprint for each chunk +\nthe entire file\n→ 2️⃣ Client checks if a file with the same fingerprint already exists with status\n\"uploading\" → if yes, resume by fetching existing chunk statuses\n→ 3️⃣ If file doesn't exist, client POSTs to initiate a multipart upload:\n        → 🔧 Backend calls S3's CreateMultipartUpload API to get an uploadId\n        → 🔑 Generates presigned URLs for EACH chunk\n        → 💾 Saves file metadata with status \"uploading\"\n        → 📤 Returns uploadId + presigned URLs to client\n→ 4️⃣ Client uploads each chunk directly to S3 using its own presigned URL (each\nrequires uploadId + partNumber)\n        → 📤 After each chunk, client sends PATCH with chunk status + ETag\n        → 🔍 Backend verifies with S3's ListParts API before marking chunk as \"uploaded\"\n→ 5️⃣ Once all chunks marked \"uploaded\", backend calls S3's CompleteMultipartUpload\nwith all part numbers + ETags\n        → 🧩 S3 assembles all parts into a single object\n        → ✅ Only after S3 confirms assembly does backend update metadata status to\n\"uploaded\"\n→ 6️⃣ Throughout the entire process, client tracks chunk progress and updates the UI so\nthe user always knows how far along they are\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How can you support large files?\n\n2 ways to do chunking,\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Client PATCH Request\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Client chunks the file and uploads chunks directly to S3\n→ S3 responds to each chunk upload with a success message\n→ Client sends a PATCH request to backend to update the chunks field in FileMetadata\n\nPATCH /files/{fileId}/chunks\nRequest:\n{\n  \"chunks\": [\n    { \"id\": \"chunk1\", \"status\": \"uploaded\" }\n  ]\n}\n\n→ Simple to implement\n→ Real-time progress tracking on the client\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Security risk, trusting the client to keep chunks in sync\n→ Malicious user could send a PATCH request marking all chunks as uploaded without actually uploading them\n→ Leads to inconsistent state that is difficult to debug\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Server-Side Chunk Verification via ETags\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Each chunk gets an ETag upon successful S3 upload\n→ Client includes ETags in the PATCH request to backend\n→ Backend verifies ETags by calling S3's ListParts API — efficiently validates multiple chunks at once\n→ Trust but verify — accept client updates for real-time progress, but verify server-side before marking file as \"uploaded\"\n\n\n→ Balances UX with data integrity\n→ Server-side verification prevents malicious or incorrect chunk status updates\n→ ETags provide a reliable way to verify chunks without re-downloading them\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ More complex than client-only approach\n→ Requires integration with S3's multipart upload API\n\n\n🔑 Uniquely Identifying Files & Chunks\n\n🤔 The Problem\nWhen resuming an upload, two questions must be answered:\n→ 1️⃣ Have I tried to upload this file before?\n→ 2️⃣ If yes, which chunks have I already uploaded?\n→ ⚠️ Cannot rely on filename alone — two users (or same user) could upload files with the same name\n\n\n🔢 Solution: Fingerprinting\n\n→ A fingerprint is a unique hash value derived from the file's content using a cryptographic hash function like SHA-256\n→ Same content always produces the same fingerprint — regardless of filename or upload source\n→ Used for deduplication (has this file been uploaded before?) and resumability (which chunks are done?)\n→ Fingerprint identifies content, not the file record — two users uploading the same file produce the same fingerprint\n→ fileId in metadata should be a UUID (unique per record), fingerprint stored as a separate field\n→ Each individual chunk also gets its own fingerprint — allows precise identification of which parts have been uploaded\n\n\n\n🔄 Full Large File Upload Flow\n\n→ 1️⃣ Client chunks file into 5-10MB pieces and calculates a fingerprint for each chunk + the entire file\n→ 2️⃣ Client checks if a file with the same fingerprint already exists with status \"uploading\" → if yes, resume by fetching existing chunk statuses\n→ 3️⃣ If file doesn't exist, client POSTs to initiate a multipart upload:\n        → 🔧 Backend calls S3's CreateMultipartUpload API to get an uploadId\n        → 🔑 Generates presigned URLs for EACH chunk\n        → 💾 Saves file metadata with status \"uploading\"\n        → 📤 Returns uploadId + presigned URLs to client\n→ 4️⃣ Client uploads each chunk directly to S3 using its own presigned URL (each requires uploadId + partNumber)\n        → 📤 After each chunk, client sends PATCH with chunk status + ETag\n        → 🔍 Backend verifies with S3's ListParts API before marking chunk as \"uploaded\"\n→ 5️⃣ Once all chunks marked \"uploaded\", backend calls S3's CompleteMultipartUpload with all part numbers + ETags\n        → 🧩 S3 assembles all parts into a single object\n        → ✅ Only after S3 confirms assembly does backend update metadata status to \"uploaded\"\n→ 6️⃣ Throughout the entire process, client tracks chunk progress and updates the UI so the user always knows how far along they are\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pIPigoQ79kMTSulNiV_td",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2715.3825961149314,
      "y": 3907.01826351818,
      "width": 812.4077270768371,
      "height": 1375,
      "angle": 0,
