      "locked": false,
      "text": "2) How can we make uploads, downloads, and syncing as fast as possible?\n\n\n📦 Chunking for Sync (Delta Sync)\n\n→ When a file changes, only sync the chunks that changed — not the whole file\n→ Each chunk has a fingerprint (hash) — unchanged chunks = same fingerprint = skip ✅\n\n⚠️ Problem with fixed-size chunks\n\n→ Inserting one byte near the start shifts all subsequent chunk boundaries\n→ Every chunk after the edit gets a different fingerprint → delta sync is useless\n\n✅ Solution: Content-Defined Chunking (CDC)\n\n→ Chunk boundaries determined by file content using a rolling hash (e.g. Rabin\nfingerprinting)\n→ A small edit only affects chunks immediately surrounding the change\n→ Vast majority of chunks remain identical → efficient delta sync\n→ This is how Dropbox actually does it in practice\n\n\n🗜️ Compression\n\n→ Fewer bytes transferred = faster uploads and downloads\n→ Since uploads go directly to S3, compression is entirely client-side\n→ Client compresses before upload → S3 stores compressed bytes → client\ndecompresses after download\n→ Backend stays out of the data path entirely ✅\n\n\nWhen to compress — it depends:\n\nFile type                                        Compression ratio                        Worth it?\n\nText files                                      High (5GB → ~1GB)                         ✅ Yes\nImages/video (.png, .mp4)        Very low (maybe a few %)              ❌ Rarely\n\n→ Implement client-side logic: decide based on file type + size + network conditions\n\nCompression algorithms:\n\nAlgorithm               Speed                     Ratio                           Best for\nGzip                       Moderate                 Good                     Broad compatibility\nBrotli                     Moderate           Better than Gzip        Browser/text\nZstandard (zstd)  ⚡ Fast           Comparable to Gzip     Client-side like Dropbox\n\n→ zstd is the strongest choice for client-side compression — tunable across speed/ratio\ntradeoffs\n\n🔐 Critical: Always Compress Before Encrypting\n\n→ Encryption introduces randomness → makes data incompressible\n→ Compressing after encrypting = near-zero compression ratio\n→ Always: compress → encrypt (never the reverse) ⚠️",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2) How can we make uploads, downloads, and syncing as fast as possible?\n\n\n📦 Chunking for Sync (Delta Sync)\n\n→ When a file changes, only sync the chunks that changed — not the whole file\n→ Each chunk has a fingerprint (hash) — unchanged chunks = same fingerprint = skip ✅\n\n⚠️ Problem with fixed-size chunks\n\n→ Inserting one byte near the start shifts all subsequent chunk boundaries\n→ Every chunk after the edit gets a different fingerprint → delta sync is useless\n\n✅ Solution: Content-Defined Chunking (CDC)\n\n→ Chunk boundaries determined by file content using a rolling hash (e.g. Rabin fingerprinting)\n→ A small edit only affects chunks immediately surrounding the change\n→ Vast majority of chunks remain identical → efficient delta sync\n→ This is how Dropbox actually does it in practice\n\n\n🗜️ Compression\n\n→ Fewer bytes transferred = faster uploads and downloads\n→ Since uploads go directly to S3, compression is entirely client-side\n→ Client compresses before upload → S3 stores compressed bytes → client decompresses after download\n→ Backend stays out of the data path entirely ✅\n\n\nWhen to compress — it depends:\n\nFile type                                        Compression ratio                        Worth it?\n\nText files                                      High (5GB → ~1GB)                         ✅ Yes\nImages/video (.png, .mp4)        Very low (maybe a few %)              ❌ Rarely\n\n→ Implement client-side logic: decide based on file type + size + network conditions\n\nCompression algorithms:\n\nAlgorithm               Speed                     Ratio                           Best for\nGzip                       Moderate                 Good                     Broad compatibility\nBrotli                     Moderate           Better than Gzip        Browser/text\nZstandard (zstd)  ⚡ Fast           Comparable to Gzip     Client-side like Dropbox\n\n→ zstd is the strongest choice for client-side compression — tunable across speed/ratio tradeoffs\n\n🔐 Critical: Always Compress Before Encrypting\n\n→ Encryption introduces randomness → makes data incompressible\n→ Compressing after encrypting = near-zero compression ratio\n→ Always: compress → encrypt (never the reverse) ⚠️",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
