    {
      "id": "hkCs3Ol4LAZmVjZIAM0N9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 851.1022872776371,
      "y": 3938.1015695703286,
      "width": 810.67333984375,
      "height": 1825,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1744675483,
      "version": 2372,
      "versionNonce": 27712060,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781441326250,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "⚠️ Limitations of a Single POST Request\n\n→ ⏱️ Timeouts: web servers and clients have timeout settings to prevent indefinite\nwaiting\n→ 🧮 Quick math: 50GB file at 100Mbps = ~1.11 hours to upload\n\n                50GB × 8 bits/byte ÷ 100Mbps = 4000 seconds\n                4000 seconds ÷ 60 ÷ 60 = 1.11 hours\n\n→ 🚫 Browser & Server Limits: most services have hard payload limits e.g. Amazon API\nGateway caps at 10MB and cannot be increased\n→ 📡 Network Interruptions: if connection drops mid-upload, user must start from scratch\n→ 🙈 Poor UX: user is completely blind to upload progress, just staring at a spinning\nwheel\n\n\n\n✅ Solution: Chunking\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ ✂️ Break the file into smaller 5-10MB pieces (chunks) on the client side before sending\n→ 📤 Upload chunks one at a time or in parallel depending on network bandwidth\n→ ⚠️ Common mistake: chunking on the server defeats the purpose, you still upload the\nentire file at once to get it there in the first place\n\n\n\n📊 Progress Indicator\n\n→ 📈 Track the progress of each chunk and update the progress bar as each chunk is\nsuccessfully uploaded\n→ ✅ Much better UX than staring at a spinning wheel for an hour\n\n\n\n🔄 Resumable Uploads\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 💾 Save the state of the upload in the database in the FileMetadata table\n→ 📋 Add a chunks field to track which chunks have been uploaded and which haven't\n\n                json {\n                  \"id\": \"123\",\n                  \"name\": \"file.txt\",\n                  \"size\": 1000,\n                  \"mimeType\": \"text/plain\",\n                  \"uploadedBy\": \"user1\",\n                  \"status\": \"uploading\",\n                  \"chunks\": [\n                    { \"id\": \"chunk1\", \"status\": \"uploaded\" },\n                    { \"id\": \"chunk2\", \"status\": \"uploading\" },\n                    { \"id\": \"chunk3\", \"status\": \"not-uploaded\" }\n                  ]\n                }\n\n→ 🔁 When user resumes, check chunks field to see which chunks are missing and only\nupload those\n→ ✅ User never has to restart the entire upload from scratch\n\n\n→ ✅ Solves all limitations of a single POST request\n→ ✅ Progress indicator gives users clear feedback\n→ ✅ Resumable — no wasted uploads on network interruption\n→ ✅ Bypasses server/browser payload size limits — each chunk is well within limits\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🧩 More complex to implement — chunking logic must live on the client\n→ 🔄 Need to keep chunks field in sync with actual uploaded chunks in Blob Storage\n→ 🗄️ Slightly more database overhead — storing chunk state per file\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "y3IyYkIO3YRdtySwaxj2P",
      "originalText": "⚠️ Limitations of a Single POST Request\n\n→ ⏱️ Timeouts: web servers and clients have timeout settings to prevent indefinite waiting\n→ 🧮 Quick math: 50GB file at 100Mbps = ~1.11 hours to upload\n\n                50GB × 8 bits/byte ÷ 100Mbps = 4000 seconds\n                4000 seconds ÷ 60 ÷ 60 = 1.11 hours\n\n→ 🚫 Browser & Server Limits: most services have hard payload limits e.g. Amazon API Gateway caps at 10MB and cannot be increased\n→ 📡 Network Interruptions: if connection drops mid-upload, user must start from scratch\n→ 🙈 Poor UX: user is completely blind to upload progress, just staring at a spinning wheel\n\n\n\n✅ Solution: Chunking\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ ✂️ Break the file into smaller 5-10MB pieces (chunks) on the client side before sending\n→ 📤 Upload chunks one at a time or in parallel depending on network bandwidth\n→ ⚠️ Common mistake: chunking on the server defeats the purpose, you still upload the entire file at once to get it there in the first place\n\n\n\n📊 Progress Indicator\n\n→ 📈 Track the progress of each chunk and update the progress bar as each chunk is successfully uploaded\n→ ✅ Much better UX than staring at a spinning wheel for an hour\n\n\n\n🔄 Resumable Uploads\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 💾 Save the state of the upload in the database in the FileMetadata table\n→ 📋 Add a chunks field to track which chunks have been uploaded and which haven't\n\n                json {\n                  \"id\": \"123\",\n                  \"name\": \"file.txt\",\n                  \"size\": 1000,\n                  \"mimeType\": \"text/plain\",\n                  \"uploadedBy\": \"user1\",\n                  \"status\": \"uploading\",\n                  \"chunks\": [\n                    { \"id\": \"chunk1\", \"status\": \"uploaded\" },\n                    { \"id\": \"chunk2\", \"status\": \"uploading\" },\n                    { \"id\": \"chunk3\", \"status\": \"not-uploaded\" }\n                  ]\n                }\n\n→ 🔁 When user resumes, check chunks field to see which chunks are missing and only upload those\n→ ✅ User never has to restart the entire upload from scratch\n\n\n→ ✅ Solves all limitations of a single POST request\n→ ✅ Progress indicator gives users clear feedback\n→ ✅ Resumable — no wasted uploads on network interruption\n→ ✅ Bypasses server/browser payload size limits — each chunk is well within limits\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🧩 More complex to implement — chunking logic must live on the client\n→ 🔄 Need to keep chunks field in sync with actual uploaded chunks in Blob Storage\n→ 🗄️ Slightly more database overhead — storing chunk state per file\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
