    {
      "id": "2bPzvR5hBIL7wekahtMgT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 856.1355587555001,
      "y": 5971.047681603016,
      "width": 813.13916015625,
      "height": 875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2IV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1800731780,
      "version": 88,
      "versionNonce": 1334146820,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781454747617,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Chunked Uploads vs Downloads\n\nUpload side 🔺\n\n→ File is split into chunks → uploaded in parallel via S3 multipart upload\n→ CompleteMultipartUpload is called → S3 assembles all parts into a single object\n→ Chunk boundaries disappear — S3 just sees one file from this point on\n\nDownload side 🔻\n\n→ Client gets a single presigned URL (or CDN signed URL) for the complete file\n→ No awareness of original chunk boundaries needed\n→ Downloads work like any normal file\n\n🔀 HTTP Range Requests — The Download Equivalent\n\n→ For very large files, HTTP natively supports Range requests\n→ Client can request specific byte ranges of the file:\nRange: bytes=0-999999      ← first 1MB\nRange: bytes=1000000-1999999  ← second 1MB\n→ Enables parallel downloads of different byte ranges simultaneously\n→ Enables resuming interrupted downloads without starting over\n→ S3 and CDNs support this natively — no special server setup needed\n\n⚠️ Key distinction\n\n→ Multipart upload chunks = defined by the uploader, tied to S3's assembly process\n→ Range request chunks = defined by the downloader, arbitrary byte boundaries,\nindependent of how the file was uploaded\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ Chunking on upload is a coordination protocol between client and S3 — once\nassembled, it's just a file; chunking on download is handled transparently via HTTP Range\nrequests, client-side and on demand.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "DmIRymBrkxfkqype2EHwu",
      "originalText": "Chunked Uploads vs Downloads\n\nUpload side 🔺\n\n→ File is split into chunks → uploaded in parallel via S3 multipart upload\n→ CompleteMultipartUpload is called → S3 assembles all parts into a single object\n→ Chunk boundaries disappear — S3 just sees one file from this point on\n\nDownload side 🔻\n\n→ Client gets a single presigned URL (or CDN signed URL) for the complete file\n→ No awareness of original chunk boundaries needed\n→ Downloads work like any normal file\n\n🔀 HTTP Range Requests — The Download Equivalent\n\n→ For very large files, HTTP natively supports Range requests\n→ Client can request specific byte ranges of the file:\nRange: bytes=0-999999      ← first 1MB\nRange: bytes=1000000-1999999  ← second 1MB\n→ Enables parallel downloads of different byte ranges simultaneously\n→ Enables resuming interrupted downloads without starting over\n→ S3 and CDNs support this natively — no special server setup needed\n\n⚠️ Key distinction\n\n→ Multipart upload chunks = defined by the uploader, tied to S3's assembly process\n→ Range request chunks = defined by the downloader, arbitrary byte boundaries, independent of how the file was uploaded\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ Chunking on upload is a coordination protocol between client and S3 — once assembled, it's just a file; chunking on download is handled transparently via HTTP Range requests, client-side and on demand.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Lm_03pr_eFEx2GGfTsUu3",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": -133.93619042288537,
