    {
      "id": "WMyLBA3kF34CJH1pK1GiU",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -128.93619042288537,
      "y": 5995.355261570588,
      "width": 799.019287109375,
      "height": 975,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 480116395,
      "version": 2324,
      "versionNonce": 344202244,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781442855716,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An ETag (Entity Tag) is essentially a fingerprint for a piece of data.\n\n\nWhat it is\n→ A short string (usually a hash) that uniquely identifies a specific version of a resource\n→ S3 generates one automatically when you upload a chunk — it's typically the MD5\nhash of the uploaded bytes\n→ If the bytes change, the ETag changes\n\n\nHow S3 uses it in multipart uploads\n\n→ You upload a chunk → S3 returns an ETag for that chunk\n→ Later, you call ListParts → S3 returns all ETags for all uploaded chunks\n→ You compare client-reported ETags vs S3's ETags → mismatch = chunk is missing or\ncorrupted\n\n\nWhy it's useful for verification\n\n→ You don't need to re-download the chunk to verify it arrived correctly\n→ Just compare the hash — if it matches, the bytes are identical ✅\n→ Cheap to compute, cheap to compare\n\n\n⚠️ One caveat with S3 ETags\n\n→ For multipart uploads, S3's ETag is not a pure MD5 of the whole file\n→ It's computed as MD5 of the concatenated part MD5s, suffixed with -N (number of\nparts)\n→ e.g. d8e8fca2dc0f896fd7cb4cb0031ba249-3\n→ So don't rely on S3 ETags to verify the final assembled file's MD5 — they're only\nreliable per-chunk\n\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ An ETag is a hash fingerprint S3 assigns to each uploaded chunk — lets you verify the\nchunk arrived intact without re-downloading it 🔍",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "Lm_03pr_eFEx2GGfTsUu3",
      "originalText": "An ETag (Entity Tag) is essentially a fingerprint for a piece of data.\n\n\nWhat it is\n→ A short string (usually a hash) that uniquely identifies a specific version of a resource\n→ S3 generates one automatically when you upload a chunk — it's typically the MD5 hash of the uploaded bytes\n→ If the bytes change, the ETag changes\n\n\nHow S3 uses it in multipart uploads\n\n→ You upload a chunk → S3 returns an ETag for that chunk\n→ Later, you call ListParts → S3 returns all ETags for all uploaded chunks\n→ You compare client-reported ETags vs S3's ETags → mismatch = chunk is missing or corrupted\n\n\nWhy it's useful for verification\n\n→ You don't need to re-download the chunk to verify it arrived correctly\n→ Just compare the hash — if it matches, the bytes are identical ✅\n→ Cheap to compute, cheap to compare\n\n\n⚠️ One caveat with S3 ETags\n\n→ For multipart uploads, S3's ETag is not a pure MD5 of the whole file\n→ It's computed as MD5 of the concatenated part MD5s, suffixed with -N (number of parts)\n→ e.g. d8e8fca2dc0f896fd7cb4cb0031ba249-3\n→ So don't rely on S3 ETags to verify the final assembled file's MD5 — they're only reliable per-chunk\n\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ An ETag is a hash fingerprint S3 assigns to each uploaded chunk — lets you verify the chunk arrived intact without re-downloading it 🔍",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "drsrvDXe_9StgowHKlSNW",
