      "text": "High-Level Design",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "High-Level Design",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "K5r0x9-HJSPgLn7QZyYTt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3045.6032759875343,
      "y": -1250.267221555784,
      "width": 982.8568384690963,
      "height": 1600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2Y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1706078166,
      "version": 343,
      "versionNonce": 449751684,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781454812338,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2) Users should be able to download a file from any device\n\n\n\n❌ Bad Solution: Download Through File Server\n\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 📥 File downloaded from Blob Storage to backend server first\n→ 📤 Then downloaded again from backend server to client\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🔁 File is downloaded twice — redundant and wasteful\n→ 🐢 Slow — double the download time\n→ 💸 Expensive — double the bandwidth costs\n→ Puts unnecessary load on backend server\n\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Download Directly from Blob Storage via Presigned URLs\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔑 Backend generates a presigned URL — temporary URL giving user permission to download from a\nspecific Blob Storage location\n→ ⚡ File goes straight from Blob Storage to client — backend never touches the file\n→ 📋 2-Step Process\n        → 1️⃣ Client requests presigned download URL from backend\n                    GET /files/{fileId}/presigned-url -> PresignedUrl\n        → 2️⃣ Client downloads file directly from Blob Storage using the presigned URL\n\n→ ✅ No redundant downloads — much faster and cheaper than bad solution\n→ ✅ Reduces load on backend server\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🌍 Blob Storage is in a single region — users far away will experience slower download times\n→ Not optimal for a large, geographically distributed user base\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Download from CDN\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🌐 Use a Content Delivery Network (CDN) — a globally distributed network of servers that cache files\nand serve them from the server closest to the user\n→ ⚡ Drastically reduces latency for geographically distributed users\n→ 🔑 Generate a signed CDN URL (like presigned URLs) giving user temporary permission to download\nfrom the CDN\n\n→ ✅ Fastest solution — file served from server closest to the user\n→ ✅ Reduces load on Blob Storage and backend\n→ ✅ Scales well for large, globally distributed user bases\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 💸 CDNs are relatively expensive\n→ 🧠 Need to be strategic about what gets cached and for how long:\n→ Use cache-control headers to specify cache duration\n→ Use cache invalidation to remove files from CDN when updated or deleted\n→ 💡 Only frequently accessed files should be cached to avoid wasting money",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2) Users should be able to download a file from any device\n\n\n\n❌ Bad Solution: Download Through File Server\n\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 📥 File downloaded from Blob Storage to backend server first\n→ 📤 Then downloaded again from backend server to client\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🔁 File is downloaded twice — redundant and wasteful\n→ 🐢 Slow — double the download time\n→ 💸 Expensive — double the bandwidth costs\n→ Puts unnecessary load on backend server\n\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Download Directly from Blob Storage via Presigned URLs\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔑 Backend generates a presigned URL — temporary URL giving user permission to download from a specific Blob Storage location\n→ ⚡ File goes straight from Blob Storage to client — backend never touches the file\n→ 📋 2-Step Process\n        → 1️⃣ Client requests presigned download URL from backend\n                    GET /files/{fileId}/presigned-url -> PresignedUrl\n        → 2️⃣ Client downloads file directly from Blob Storage using the presigned URL\n\n→ ✅ No redundant downloads — much faster and cheaper than bad solution\n→ ✅ Reduces load on backend server\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🌍 Blob Storage is in a single region — users far away will experience slower download times\n→ Not optimal for a large, geographically distributed user base\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Download from CDN\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🌐 Use a Content Delivery Network (CDN) — a globally distributed network of servers that cache files and serve them from the server closest to the user\n→ ⚡ Drastically reduces latency for geographically distributed users\n→ 🔑 Generate a signed CDN URL (like presigned URLs) giving user temporary permission to download from the CDN\n\n→ ✅ Fastest solution — file served from server closest to the user\n→ ✅ Reduces load on Blob Storage and backend\n→ ✅ Scales well for large, globally distributed user bases\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 💸 CDNs are relatively expensive\n→ 🧠 Need to be strategic about what gets cached and for how long:\n→ Use cache-control headers to specify cache duration\n→ Use cache invalidation to remove files from CDN when updated or deleted\n→ 💡 Only frequently accessed files should be cached to avoid wasting money",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Dndc_m14HORCx-eHQz4xy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4214.369726511843,
      "y": -1258.7210195829612,
      "width": 959.1411891049721,
      "height": 1850,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2Z",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1421127830,
      "version": 746,
      "versionNonce": 1742406588,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781382820037,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3) Users should be able to share a file with other users\n\n\n\n❌ Bad Solution: Add a Sharelist to File Metadata\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 📋 Add a list of users who have access directly to the file metadata\n→ When a file is shared, add the user to the sharelist\n→ When a file is downloaded, check if the user is in the sharelist\n        json{\n          \"id\": \"123\",\n          \"name\": \"file.txt\",\n          \"size\": 1000,\n          \"mimeType\": \"text/plain\",\n          \"uploadedBy\": \"user1\",\n          \"sharelist\": [\"user2\", \"user3\"]\n        }\n→ Simple and easy to implement\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🔍 Getting a user's own files is fast — query using index on uploadedBy\n→ 🐢 Getting files shared with a user is slow — must scan the sharelist of every single file to check if the\nuser is in it\n→ Gets worse as the number of files grows\n\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Cache the Inverse Sharelist\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Keep the sharelist in file metadata and maintain a cache of the inverse relationship\n→ 🗺️ Cache maps each user → list of files shared with them\n→ ⚡ When a user opens the app, instantly look up their userId in the cache to get all shared files\nuser1 → [\"fileId1\", \"fileId2\"]\n→ ✅ Much faster lookup for shared files — simple key-value cache lookup\n→ No full table scan needed\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🔄 Need to keep the cache in sync with the sharelist in file metadata\n→ 🔐 Solved by updating both the sharelist and cache in a single transaction in the same database\n→ More complex than the bad solution — two sources of truth to manage\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Separate SharedFiles Table\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 📊 Fully normalize the data by creating a dedicated SharedFiles table\n→ Maps userId → fileId for every shared file\n→ When a user opens the app, query SharedFiles table for all rows where userId matches\n\n                👤 userId (Partition Key)         📁 fileId (Sort Key)\n                        user1                                            fileId1\n                        user1                                            fileId2\n                        user2                                            fileId3\n\n→ 🔑 userId + fileId form a composite primary key — allows same user to have multiple files shared with\nthem\n→ No longer need the sharelist in file metadata — SharedFiles table is the single source of truth\n\n→ ✅ Clean, normalized data — no duplication or sync issues\n→ ✅ Simple to query — just filter SharedFiles by userId\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Slightly less efficient than cache approach — index query vs simple key-value lookup\n→ 🧩 Requires a schema change and a new table\n→ 💡 Tradeoff is usually worth it — simpler data model and no sync complexity",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3) Users should be able to share a file with other users\n\n\n\n❌ Bad Solution: Add a Sharelist to File Metadata\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 📋 Add a list of users who have access directly to the file metadata\n→ When a file is shared, add the user to the sharelist\n→ When a file is downloaded, check if the user is in the sharelist\n        json{\n          \"id\": \"123\",\n          \"name\": \"file.txt\",\n          \"size\": 1000,\n          \"mimeType\": \"text/plain\",\n          \"uploadedBy\": \"user1\",\n          \"sharelist\": [\"user2\", \"user3\"]\n        }\n→ Simple and easy to implement\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🔍 Getting a user's own files is fast — query using index on uploadedBy\n→ 🐢 Getting files shared with a user is slow — must scan the sharelist of every single file to check if the user is in it\n→ Gets worse as the number of files grows\n\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Cache the Inverse Sharelist\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Keep the sharelist in file metadata and maintain a cache of the inverse relationship\n→ 🗺️ Cache maps each user → list of files shared with them\n→ ⚡ When a user opens the app, instantly look up their userId in the cache to get all shared files\nuser1 → [\"fileId1\", \"fileId2\"]\n→ ✅ Much faster lookup for shared files — simple key-value cache lookup\n→ No full table scan needed\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🔄 Need to keep the cache in sync with the sharelist in file metadata\n→ 🔐 Solved by updating both the sharelist and cache in a single transaction in the same database\n→ More complex than the bad solution — two sources of truth to manage\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Separate SharedFiles Table\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 📊 Fully normalize the data by creating a dedicated SharedFiles table\n→ Maps userId → fileId for every shared file\n→ When a user opens the app, query SharedFiles table for all rows where userId matches\n\n                👤 userId (Partition Key)         📁 fileId (Sort Key)\n                        user1                                            fileId1\n                        user1                                            fileId2\n                        user2                                            fileId3\n\n→ 🔑 userId + fileId form a composite primary key — allows same user to have multiple files shared with them\n→ No longer need the sharelist in file metadata — SharedFiles table is the single source of truth\n\n→ ✅ Clean, normalized data — no duplication or sync issues\n→ ✅ Simple to query — just filter SharedFiles by userId\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Slightly less efficient than cache approach — index query vs simple key-value lookup\n→ 🧩 Requires a schema change and a new table\n→ 💡 Tradeoff is usually worth it — simpler data model and no sync complexity",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "-_7FxaInhdfoC7EDO4GJa",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5370.383259048902,
      "y": -1267.3949590196953,
      "width": 982.8568384690963,
      "height": 2400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2a",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1799040330,
      "version": 825,
      "versionNonce": 419511300,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781385921196,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "4) Users can automatically sync files across devices\n\n\n🔄 File Sync Across Devices\nTwo directions to sync:\n\n→ 📤 Local → Remote (user edits file on device, push to cloud)\n→ 📥 Remote → Local (cloud changes, pull to device)\n\n\n\n📤 Local → Remote\n\n🔧 Approach: Client-Side Sync Agent\n\n→ 👀 Monitors local Dropbox folder for changes using OS-specific file system events:\n→ FileSystemWatcher on Windows\n→ FSEvents on macOS\n→ When change detected, queues the modified file for upload locally\n→ Uses upload API to send changes to server along with updated metadata\n→ ⚔️ Conflicts resolved using \"last write wins\" — most recent edit is saved\n\n\n📝 Note on Versioning\n\n→ Out of scope here, but typically you wouldn't overwrite the existing file\n→ Instead, add a new file (or new chunks) and update a version number and pointer on the metadata\n→ Remote server is the single source of truth — changes propagated as soon as possible\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ ⚔️ Last write wins is simple but can cause data loss if two users edit simultaneously\n→ OS-specific file system events add complexity across platforms\n\n\n\n📥 Remote → Local\nEach client needs to know when remote changes happen so it can pull them down. Three approaches:\n\n\n\n❌ Bad Solution: Polling\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ ⏰ Client periodically asks the server \"has anything changed since my last sync?\"\n→ Server queries DB for files with updatedAt timestamp newer than last sync time\n→ Simple to implement\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🐢 Slow to detect changes — only as fast as the polling interval\n→ Wastes bandwidth — sends requests even when nothing has changed\n→ Doesn't scale well with many connected clients\n\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: WebSocket or SSE\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔌 Server maintains an open connection with each client\n→ Pushes notifications to client when changes occur in real-time\n→ ✅ Real-time updates — near instant sync\n→ No wasted bandwidth — server only sends data when something changes\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ More complex to implement than polling\n→ 💀 WebSocket connections can drop — missed messages mean missed updates\n→ No guaranteed delivery — not reliable enough on its own\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Hybrid (WebSocket + Polling)\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔌 Each client maintains a single WebSocket (or SSE) connection per device/session — not one per file\n→ Server pushes change notifications for all files the user has access to through this connection\n→ 🛡️ Polling acts as a safety net to catch any missed changes\n→ ⚡ Active Notification — server pushes change events through WebSocket in real-time as they happen\n→ 🔁 Periodic Polling — client also polls every few minutes using:\n\n        GET /files/changes?since={timestamp}\n\n→ catches any changes missed due to dropped WebSocket connections\n\n→ ✅ Best of both worlds — real-time updates with a reliable fallback\n→ ✅ Guarantees eventual consistency even if WebSocket was temporarily interrupted\n→ ✅ Single connection per device — efficient and scalable\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🧩 Most complex of the three approaches\n→ ⚙️ Need to manage both WebSocket connections and polling logic on the client",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "4) Users can automatically sync files across devices\n\n\n🔄 File Sync Across Devices\nTwo directions to sync:\n\n→ 📤 Local → Remote (user edits file on device, push to cloud)\n→ 📥 Remote → Local (cloud changes, pull to device)\n\n\n\n📤 Local → Remote\n\n🔧 Approach: Client-Side Sync Agent\n\n→ 👀 Monitors local Dropbox folder for changes using OS-specific file system events:\n→ FileSystemWatcher on Windows\n→ FSEvents on macOS\n→ When change detected, queues the modified file for upload locally\n→ Uses upload API to send changes to server along with updated metadata\n→ ⚔️ Conflicts resolved using \"last write wins\" — most recent edit is saved\n\n\n📝 Note on Versioning\n\n→ Out of scope here, but typically you wouldn't overwrite the existing file\n→ Instead, add a new file (or new chunks) and update a version number and pointer on the metadata\n→ Remote server is the single source of truth — changes propagated as soon as possible\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ ⚔️ Last write wins is simple but can cause data loss if two users edit simultaneously\n→ OS-specific file system events add complexity across platforms\n\n\n\n📥 Remote → Local\nEach client needs to know when remote changes happen so it can pull them down. Three approaches:\n\n\n\n❌ Bad Solution: Polling\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ ⏰ Client periodically asks the server \"has anything changed since my last sync?\"\n→ Server queries DB for files with updatedAt timestamp newer than last sync time\n→ Simple to implement\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🐢 Slow to detect changes — only as fast as the polling interval\n→ Wastes bandwidth — sends requests even when nothing has changed\n→ Doesn't scale well with many connected clients\n\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: WebSocket or SSE\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔌 Server maintains an open connection with each client\n→ Pushes notifications to client when changes occur in real-time\n→ ✅ Real-time updates — near instant sync\n→ No wasted bandwidth — server only sends data when something changes\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ More complex to implement than polling\n→ 💀 WebSocket connections can drop — missed messages mean missed updates\n→ No guaranteed delivery — not reliable enough on its own\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Hybrid (WebSocket + Polling)\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔌 Each client maintains a single WebSocket (or SSE) connection per device/session — not one per file\n→ Server pushes change notifications for all files the user has access to through this connection\n→ 🛡️ Polling acts as a safety net to catch any missed changes\n→ ⚡ Active Notification — server pushes change events through WebSocket in real-time as they happen\n→ 🔁 Periodic Polling — client also polls every few minutes using:\n\n        GET /files/changes?since={timestamp}\n\n→ catches any changes missed due to dropped WebSocket connections\n\n→ ✅ Best of both worlds — real-time updates with a reliable fallback\n→ ✅ Guarantees eventual consistency even if WebSocket was temporarily interrupted\n→ ✅ Single connection per device — efficient and scalable\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ 🧩 Most complex of the three approaches\n→ ⚙️ Need to manage both WebSocket connections and polling logic on the client",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
