      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1840896772,
      "version": 602,
      "versionNonce": 1962468540,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781441272839,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Parallel Chunked Uploads & TCP Bandwidth Utilization\n\n🧩 Why Chunking + Parallel Uploads Is Faster\n\n→ A single large upload = one sequential TCP stream, one connection\n→ Chunking splits the file → multiple chunks uploaded simultaneously over multiple TCP connections\n→ Combined throughput of parallel connections gets closer to your actual bandwidth ceiling\n\nBenefits beyond speed:\n\n→ Resume on failure — only retry the failed chunk, not the whole file ✅\n→ Server-side parallelism — S3 multipart upload processes chunks in parallel on their end too ✅\n\n\n\n📉 Why a Single TCP Connection Underutilizes Bandwidth\n\nCongestion Window (CWND)\n\n→ TCP limits how much data can be \"in flight\" (sent but unacknowledged) at once\n→ Throughput is capped at:\n        Throughput ≈ CWND size / RTT\n→ High RTT (uploading to a distant server) = low throughput even on fast connections — you're\nwaiting for ACKs before sending more\n\nSlow Start\n\n→ New TCP connections don't open at full speed — window starts small and doubles every RTT\n→ Wastes time ramping up, especially for large files\n\nPacket Loss\n\n→ On loss, TCP assumes congestion and cuts window size significantly\n→ Results in a sawtooth pattern — constantly growing and shrinking, never fully utilizing bandwidth\n\n\n🛣️ Why Multiple Connections Help\n\n→ Each chunk upload = its own TCP connection with its own independent CWND\n→ Instead of one window throttling and reacting to loss, you have 4–8 windows sending\nsimultaneously\n→ Like a highway — one car is limited by traffic, but 8 cars across 8 lanes move far more total cargo\n\n→ Single connection: may only sustain 30–40 Mbps on a 100 Mbps line due to window limits\n→ Parallel connections: collectively push much closer to the 100 Mbps ceiling ✅\n\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs & Limits\n\n\n                Scenario                                                           Chunking helps?\n\nHigh bandwidth, high RTT                                 ✅ Significant speedup\nAlready saturating upload bandwidth              ❌ No gain — ISP limit is the ceiling\nLossy network                                                      ✅ Other chunks keep flowing while one retries\nToo many parallel chunks                                   ⚠️ Overhead and congestion can hurt performance\n\n\n→ Real-world speedup depends on: network conditions, server limits, and chunk size tuning\n→ Sweet spot typically 4–8 parallel connections — beyond that, gains diminish\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ A single TCP connection underutilizes bandwidth because congestion control, slow start, and\npacket loss keep throttling it — parallel chunk uploads bypass this by running multiple independent\nwindows simultaneously, collectively saturating your actual bandwidth ceiling.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "HREoolNVHTehWg8G8mNCO",
      "originalText": "Parallel Chunked Uploads & TCP Bandwidth Utilization\n\n🧩 Why Chunking + Parallel Uploads Is Faster\n\n→ A single large upload = one sequential TCP stream, one connection\n→ Chunking splits the file → multiple chunks uploaded simultaneously over multiple TCP connections\n→ Combined throughput of parallel connections gets closer to your actual bandwidth ceiling\n\nBenefits beyond speed:\n\n→ Resume on failure — only retry the failed chunk, not the whole file ✅\n→ Server-side parallelism — S3 multipart upload processes chunks in parallel on their end too ✅\n\n\n\n📉 Why a Single TCP Connection Underutilizes Bandwidth\n\nCongestion Window (CWND)\n\n→ TCP limits how much data can be \"in flight\" (sent but unacknowledged) at once\n→ Throughput is capped at:\n        Throughput ≈ CWND size / RTT\n→ High RTT (uploading to a distant server) = low throughput even on fast connections — you're waiting for ACKs before sending more\n\nSlow Start\n\n→ New TCP connections don't open at full speed — window starts small and doubles every RTT\n→ Wastes time ramping up, especially for large files\n\nPacket Loss\n\n→ On loss, TCP assumes congestion and cuts window size significantly\n→ Results in a sawtooth pattern — constantly growing and shrinking, never fully utilizing bandwidth\n\n\n🛣️ Why Multiple Connections Help\n\n→ Each chunk upload = its own TCP connection with its own independent CWND\n→ Instead of one window throttling and reacting to loss, you have 4–8 windows sending simultaneously\n→ Like a highway — one car is limited by traffic, but 8 cars across 8 lanes move far more total cargo\n\n→ Single connection: may only sustain 30–40 Mbps on a 100 Mbps line due to window limits\n→ Parallel connections: collectively push much closer to the 100 Mbps ceiling ✅\n\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs & Limits\n\n\n                Scenario                                                           Chunking helps?\n\nHigh bandwidth, high RTT                                 ✅ Significant speedup\nAlready saturating upload bandwidth              ❌ No gain — ISP limit is the ceiling\nLossy network                                                      ✅ Other chunks keep flowing while one retries\nToo many parallel chunks                                   ⚠️ Overhead and congestion can hurt performance\n\n\n→ Real-world speedup depends on: network conditions, server limits, and chunk size tuning\n→ Sweet spot typically 4–8 parallel connections — beyond that, gains diminish\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ A single TCP connection underutilizes bandwidth because congestion control, slow start, and packet loss keep throttling it — parallel chunk uploads bypass this by running multiple independent windows simultaneously, collectively saturating your actual bandwidth ceiling.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "d4weaKiul0dLU2As5gn7N",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3612.258721637693,
      "y": 3898.4608627127536,
      "width": 877.6203452941454,
      "height": 1175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
