      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781456352027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3) How can you ensure file security?\n\n\n🔒 Encryption in Transit\n\n→ Use HTTPS for all client-server communication — standard, supported everywhere\n→ Protects data as it moves between client and server\n\n🗄️ Encryption at Rest\n\n→ S3 encrypts each file with a unique key, stored separately from the file\n→ Easy to enable — specified at upload time\n→ Even if someone gains raw access to S3 storage, they can't decrypt without the key ✅\n\n👥 Access Control\n\n→ Share table / ACL controls who is authorized to request a download link\n→ But: what if an authorized user leaks the link publicly? ⚠️\n\n\n✅ Solution: Signed URLs\n\n→ Short-lived URLs generated server-side (e.g. 5 minute expiry)\n→ Anyone with a valid, unexpired URL can download — they are bearer tokens ⚠️\n→ Short expiry window limits exposure but doesn't fully prevent sharing\n\nFor higher security, add:\n\n→ IP binding — URL only works from the requestor's IP\n→ Auth cookie requirement — signed URL must be used alongside a valid session\n\n🔑 How Signed URLs Work (e.g. CloudFront + S3)\n\n→ Generation — server creates URL with signature incorporating: path + expiry timestamp +\noptional restrictions (IP etc.), signed with provider's private key\n→ Distribution — signed URL sent to authorized user only\n→ Validation — CDN verifies signature using registered public key, checks expiry and restrictions\n→ serves or denies\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\nApproach                                        Security                                                      Convenience\n\nLong-lived URLs                   ❌ Leak = permanent exposure                           ✅ Simple\nShort-lived signed URLs      ✅ Limits exposure window              ⚠️ Must re-generate frequently\nIP-bound signed URLs         ✅✅ Harder to share                         ⚠️ Breaks for mobile/VPN users\nSigned URL + auth cookie   ✅✅ Strongest                                   ⚠️ Most complex to implement",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3) How can you ensure file security?\n\n\n🔒 Encryption in Transit\n\n→ Use HTTPS for all client-server communication — standard, supported everywhere\n→ Protects data as it moves between client and server\n\n🗄️ Encryption at Rest\n\n→ S3 encrypts each file with a unique key, stored separately from the file\n→ Easy to enable — specified at upload time\n→ Even if someone gains raw access to S3 storage, they can't decrypt without the key ✅\n\n👥 Access Control\n\n→ Share table / ACL controls who is authorized to request a download link\n→ But: what if an authorized user leaks the link publicly? ⚠️\n\n\n✅ Solution: Signed URLs\n\n→ Short-lived URLs generated server-side (e.g. 5 minute expiry)\n→ Anyone with a valid, unexpired URL can download — they are bearer tokens ⚠️\n→ Short expiry window limits exposure but doesn't fully prevent sharing\n\nFor higher security, add:\n\n→ IP binding — URL only works from the requestor's IP\n→ Auth cookie requirement — signed URL must be used alongside a valid session\n\n🔑 How Signed URLs Work (e.g. CloudFront + S3)\n\n→ Generation — server creates URL with signature incorporating: path + expiry timestamp + optional restrictions (IP etc.), signed with provider's private key\n→ Distribution — signed URL sent to authorized user only\n→ Validation — CDN verifies signature using registered public key, checks expiry and restrictions → serves or denies\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\nApproach                                        Security                                                      Convenience\n\nLong-lived URLs                   ❌ Leak = permanent exposure                           ✅ Simple\nShort-lived signed URLs      ✅ Limits exposure window              ⚠️ Must re-generate frequently\nIP-bound signed URLs         ✅✅ Harder to share                         ⚠️ Breaks for mobile/VPN users\nSigned URL + auth cookie   ✅✅ Strongest                                   ⚠️ Most complex to implement",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
